n\n$$\n(1+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{1+1}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(k+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+1}\n$$\n\nThen, we move on to the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((k+1)+2)^{2} & \\le {3}^{(k+1)+1} \\\\ (k+3)^{2} & \\le {3}^{k+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives:\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+3)^{2}\\le {3}^{k+2}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this inequality using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than or equal to the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186413946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218641